00:01:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're absolutely right that the core answer is that simple: people tried things, and if it killed them, others learned not to eat it. That *is* the basic mechanism. I'm sorry if my first answer made it sound like I was overcomplicating what is, at its heart, a straightforward trial-and-error story. I gave a detailed answer because your original question "Did someone just take one for the team?" touches on a fascinating point: we often imagine some poor lone caveman dropping dead after a single bite of a shiny berry. But if that was the *only* method—just blind, all-or-nothing fatal tests—we would have lost an awful lot of "teammates" and likely wouldn't have survived as a species. So while you're right that the basic rule is "unsafe = can kill you," the interesting part of the story is how we managed to figure out so much without everyone dropping dead constantly. The "complicated" part was just explaining the clever, low-risk tricks we used to avoid the "kills you" part in the first place. Think of it this way: the simple rule is "unsafe things are deadly," but the real, on-the-ground process was "unsafe things will make you vomit, tingle, or burn *before* they kill you, so we paid attention to those smaller warnings." That's not a different truth, it's just the step that made the simple rule survivable. So yes, absolutely: people just tried stuff, and if they didn't die, it went on the menu. I only added the "how they managed to try stuff without dying" bit because it makes the story less about suicidal bravery and more about careful, smart survival. I hope that clears it up.
